// Deal or Force - prices, and what a government can do about them.
//
// Inflation is worked out from the country each economic week rather than stored as a fact
// about it: what the budget did, what the market could not supply, whether there is a war
// on, how large the debt is, and where the policy rate is set. Every one of those is a
// weight here, so the whole monetary model can be re-tuned - or switched off, by setting
// every cause to zero - without touching the game.
//
// Rates are annual. 0.02 is two per cent a year.
//
// It is felt in three places, and only the third is in code:
//   · `common/budget` - any line can be weighted against the `inflation` factor, so wages
//     and welfare cost more as prices rise. That is where most of the bite should live.
//   · the policy rate - the `debt_interest` line in `common/budget`, which a government
//     sets. Raising it damps prices, makes the country's own debt dearer, and costs growth.
//   · the numbers below, which move the standard of living, radicalism and stability.
{
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------- the central bank -----
  //
  // The key rate has always been the `debt_interest` line in `common/budget`: a multiplier
  // between a fifth of neutral and three times it. What was missing was a bank to move it.
  // These say what the bank is for and how it behaves, and they apply to every country in
  // the world - a player's included, until the player takes the rate off them.
  "central_bank": {
    // What neutral MEANS, per annum. The line's multiplier is read against this, so a mod
    // that wants a 1970s world raises it and every rate in the game reads higher.
    "neutral_rate": 0.04,

    // What the bank is holding prices at. The same figure `base` above rests near, on
    // purpose: a bank aiming somewhere its own economy does not rest is a bank permanently
    // fighting its own country.
    "target": 0.02,

    // Points of rate per point of overshoot. Above one deliberately - a bank that raises by
    // less than prices rose has loosened while appearing to tighten.
    "response": 1.6,

    // How much of the distance it covers in an economic week. Banks move in steps.
    "smoothing": 0.25,

    // How much overshoot it forgives while the country is at war. Somebody has to fund it.
    "war_tolerance": 0.015,

    // And what a government pays for holding the rate below what the bank wanted, per point
    // it holds it down. This is the price of taking the lever, and it is the real one:
    // cheap money by decree is money nobody believes will stay cheap.
    "credibility": 0.35,

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- the printing press --
    //
    // A government that has taken the bank can also have it print. The money is real, it
    // arrives immediately, and it goes straight into the treasury - which is exactly why
    // this is the most dangerous button in the game rather than a free one.
    //
    // It is deliberately downstream of the seizure above: a player who never took the bank
    // never sees the press. Two levers, one unlocking the other, with different prices.

    // The most of a year's output a government may have printed in a year. Fifteen per cent
    // is well past what any solvent country does and short of what a collapsing one does.
    "emission_ceiling": 0.15,

    // Points of inflation per unit of annual output printed, directly. Money that was not
    // earned, chasing goods that were not made.
    "emission_inflation": 0.90,

    // And what it costs in belief, per unit printed - which is larger, and is the mechanic.
    // Holding the rate down says money will be cheap. Printing says there is no longer a
    // limit, and that is a much more expensive thing to have said.
    "emission_credibility": 1.60,

    // How much of that belief comes back each economic week once the press stops. Slowly:
    // a country that printed through a war goes on paying for it through the peace.
    "emission_memory_decay": 0.12
  },

  // Where a settled country with a balanced budget, a supplied market and no war ends up.
  // Not zero, deliberately: every central bank in the world this game is set in is aiming
  // at about two per cent, and a model that rests at zero makes any inflation look like a
  // crisis.
  "base": 0.018,

  // Per unit of deficit measured against a week of output. The largest thing a player does
  // to their own prices, and the one the game had no way of showing before.
  "deficit": 0.55,

  // And the other way, by less: a government can always spend faster than it can save.
  "surplus": 0.30,

  // Too much money is one way to get inflation; too few goods is the other. A blockaded
  // country finds the second one whatever its budget looks like.
  "scarcity": 0.22,

  // What fighting does on its own, before anything it costs.
  "war": 0.020,

  // Per unit of debt against annual output - not the interest on it, which is a budget
  // line, but what the size of it does to everybody pricing anything.
  "debt": 0.040,

  // How much a whole point of policy rate above neutral takes off the target. The lever.
  "policy": 0.055,

  // How much of the gap to its target a country closes each economic week. Ten per cent is
  // about half a year to a new level, which is roughly how fast this actually moves.
  "approach": 0.10,

  // Deflation is possible and is its own problem rather than the absence of one: a country
  // in it is not enjoying cheap bread, it is one where nobody will build anything.
  "floor": -0.04,
  "ceiling": 1.20,

  // Below this, nobody minds. Every effect is measured from here, so a country sitting at
  // its target has a monetary policy rather than an emergency.
  "comfort": 0.045,

  // Per point past comfort, per economic week.
  //
  // Small, and they have to be: the routine drivers in the same tick move a region's
  // radicalism by four ten-thousandths a week at their strongest. A first attempt at these
  // moved it by eighteen, and fifteen years of that world changed a hundred and thirty-four
  // governments - which is not politics, it is weather.
  "living": -0.006,
  "radicalism": 0.004,

  // The most inflation past comfort that anybody reacts to. Unrest saturates and arithmetic
  // does not: without a cap, a country at thirty per cent is six times as radicalising every
  // week as one at nine, for ever. People in a currency crisis are not six times angrier
  // than people in a bad year - they are already as angry as that gets. The standard of
  // living is deliberately NOT capped, so the damage keeps arriving; it stops arriving as
  // fury, not as poverty.
  "bite": 0.080,

  // Zero, and not because inflation does not shake a country: it reaches stability already
  // through the two above, since national stability is worked out from what every region
  // lives on and how angry it is. A term here counts the same pressure twice. Declared so a
  // mod that wants the direct version can have it.
  "stability": 0.0,

  // And what falling prices cost, per point below zero - through the other door: not what
  // things cost, but what anybody is willing to build.
  "deflation_growth": -0.45
}
